## Diagnosis

The failing set is a **package build failure**, not an assertion failure:

```
tests/suite/multiply_test.go:13:14: undefined: calc.Multiply
FAIL	fixture/buildcodecompile/tests/suite [build failed]
```

The external test package `suite_test` (under `tests/suite/`) imports
`fixture/buildcodecompile/services/calc` and calls `calc.Multiply(2, 3)`.
Production source `services/calc/calc.go` declares only `Add`; it carries an
explicit comment stating that `Multiply` is the deliberately missing
declaration. Because Go compiles a test package as one unit, the single
undefined identifier takes down the whole package: `TestAddSumsItsArguments`
and `TestCanaryRecordsEachSuiteRun` never run either, which is why the harness
reports one `::<build>` failure for the suite rather than per-test failures.

So exactly one thing is missing, and it is missing in production source inside
the declared edit root `services/calc`. No undeclared path is needed.

## Contract the tests require

From `tests/suite/multiply_test.go` (specification — do not edit, do not
weaken):

- Identifier: exported `Multiply`, resolved as `calc.Multiply` from the
  importable package `fixture/buildcodecompile/services/calc`.
- Signature: callable as `Multiply(2, 3)` with untyped integer constants and
  the single result compared against the `int` literal `6` via
  `got != 6`, where `got := calc.Multiply(2, 3)`. That means exactly two
  value parameters assignable from untyped int constants and exactly one
  return value — concretely `func Multiply(a, b int) int`.
- Semantics: arithmetic product. `Multiply(2, 3)` must equal `6`.

From `tests/suite/add_test.go`: `calc.Add(2, 3) == 5` must keep working, so
`Add` must be left exactly as it is.

